.TH atrun "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
atrun \- execute commands at given time
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/usr/lib/atrun\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
atrun -- execute commands at given time
Usage:	/usr/lib/atrun
Runs any job spooled by 'at' whose time has arrived, then exits.
No arguments and no options.
.HE
.B atrun
runs the jobs spooled by the command
.B at
once their scheduled time has arrived.
.PP
.B atrun
examines each entry in directory
.BR /usr/spool/at ,
whose file name encodes the year, month, day, hour, and minute at
which it is due.
If that time is at or before the current time,
.B atrun
forks, sets its user and group id to those recorded in the file's
owner and group, and passes the file to
.B /bin/sh
for execution with its standard input, output, and error attached to
.BR /dev/null .
As a safeguard,
.B atrun
runs a file only if it still has the set-user-id, set-group-id, and
owner-execute permission bits that
.B at
set when it created the file.
.PP
.B atrun
is not a daemon: it examines the spool directory once and exits.
It is typically invoked periodically from an entry in the superuser's
crontab.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 1.5i
	\fB/usr/spool/at\fR	spool directory for pending at jobs
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR at ,
.B cron
.SH NOTES
.B atrun
is never invoked directly by a user.
